   syntax: #action [{trigger}] [{response}]
   With no arguments, prints all actions.
   With one argument, prints all actions which match the arg
   (with wildcard expansion of '*' supported).
   Otherwise, it defines an action.  Whenever lyntin sees anything incoming
   which contains the text trigger, it evaluates the text response.
   response can be any lyntin or plain text command.  When defining an
   braces ( '{' and '}' ) are mandatory around _both_ the trigger and the
   response. Actions can be 'anchored' to the beginning of a line by
   prepending the trigger with '^'. Then, the action will not be triggered
   unless it occurs at the beginning of a line.

   Actions can contain lyntin pattern-variables, which look like
   %[integer]  When lyntin sees a pattern-variable in an action trigger, it
   tries to match any pattern against it, and saves any match it finds so
   you can use it in the response. see below for examples.  response can
   contain the special variable %a, which means "the whole matched line."

   ex:
   #ac {^You are hungry} {get bread bag;eat bread}
   #ac {EVISCERATES joey} {rescue joey}
   #ac {%0 gives you %5} {say thanks for the %5, %0!}
   #ac {%1 tells you %2} {say %1 just told me %2}
